      "notes": "All claims are well-grounded. The stated claim quotes verbatim from policy text. E2 (measurable) is supported by multi-source evidence including government publications; the causal language 'has been shown to reduce' is appropriate for an established evidence base. E5 and E3 are correctly tiered as projected with appropriately hedged language ('suggests', 'estimates'). E35 is a Parliamentary committee finding and correctly measurable. E26 is an IPPR advocacy source but is labelled as such and not used as sole basis for the verdict direction \u2014 it supports a qualifying caveat rather than driving the core finding. E39 (62% figure) is from a 2020 House of Commons Library report and correctly tiered measurable. Direction 'improves/minor/long-term/low-confidence' is well-calibrated: the mechanism is indirect (prevention reducing demand), delivery risk is explicitly acknowledged, and the rationale correctly distinguishes the DfT's \u00a34.5bn strategy (E21) from what this specific policy text commits to. No lever leakage, no party identity signals, no false balance issues \u2014 the verdict does not manufacture counter-claims but acknowledges genuine delivery uncertainty. One minor observation: E26 source is IPPR (advocacy) and E36 cites IPPR similarly; both are labelled and used only for contextual hedging, not as determinative evidence, so this does not reach blocking threshold."
